Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Chrysalis-Hiss in the Coin Slot
- Story & Context: Outside the ruined arcade doors of the Starlight Galleria, nineteen-year-old Kaelen soldered a raw TR-808 trigger wire straight into a dead Pac-Man motherboard, causing the whole concourse ceiling to hum with a terrifying, beautiful low frequency. As the first subterranean sub-bass hit the empty glass storefronts, Maya stepped into the dry fountain basin and sang her first improvised verse over the crackling cabinet speakers, accidentally inaugurating the entire scene. The song captures the exact moment the mall's back-up generator surged to life, burning out three amplifiers but leaving behind a sacred, legendary room-reverb recording. She leaned into the static and sang, "Insert two quarters just to hear me break down in the dark."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-angle artwork of a glowing brass arcade coin slot resting in damp soil, with luminous blue moth wings sprouting from the coin intake opening. Atmospheric neon blue and amber light, volumetric dust, surreal analog photography, 35mm grain.

Track 2: Kitsch-Muster (The Shell Bleeds Blue)
- Story & Context: Written during a brutal February blizzard when the sub-zero temperatures inside the ghost mall froze the water lines and made the acoustic guitar strings snap under tension. Maya locked herself inside an abandoned photo-booth with a portable cassette deck, recording a tear-streaked confession about her sister running away to Chicago. Kaelen backed her vocals with a agonizingly slow 808 pulse that mimics a dying heart monitor spliced with distorted Street Fighter lose-screen sound bytes. She wept into the booth's internal intercom, softly repeating, "You left your winter coat behind the cabinet."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal album art depicting an abandoned vintage photo booth sitting in a snowy, ruined mall corridor. Inside the booth, instead of photos, long strips of translucent film float outward, displaying glowing pixelated moths trapped in glass. Dark melancholic lighting, filmic depth of field.

Track 3: Did the Maggots Eat Your Saved High Score?
- Story & Context: Years after the scene was cleared out by city contractors, this track survives as an urban legend cut from a single bootleg lathe-cut vinyl found in an attic. It features a haunted synth-melody played on an uncalibrated sound chip that drifts wildly out of tune against a wall of thunderous 808 sub-bass drops. The track ends with the actual audio of demolition crews breaking down the mall's glass atrium while Maya’s distant, heavily echoed voice fades out over a dying acoustic chord. In the final silence, her voice lingers: "We etched our names in vector light before the concrete fell."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic wide shot of a crumbling concrete arcade cabinet completely covered in blooming, iridescent wild moss and glowing white fungi. The glowing screen displays a fading pixelated game-over screen in a dark, flooded room. Gloomy, apocalyptic art style, soft focus, dramatic shadows.

